The cross-section of a prism with index of refraction  $1.5$  is an equilateral triangle, as shown above. A ray of light comes in horizontally from air into the prism, and has the opportunity to leave the prism, at an angle  $\theta$  with respect to the surface of the triangle. Find  $\theta$  in degrees and round to the nearest whole number. 

*(Ahaan Rungta, 5 points)*

There are 7 people standing in a row. How many different arrangements are there according to the following requirements?

(1) Among them, A cannot stand at the head, and B cannot stand at the tail;
(2) Among them, A, B, and C must stand next to each other;
(3) Among them, A, B, and C must not stand next to each other;
(4) There is exactly one person between A and B;
(5) A, B, and C must stand in order from left to right.

In a chess tournament for the master sportsman title, 12 players participated, with each player playing one game against every other player. A victory in a game is awarded 1 point, a draw 0.5 points, and a loss 0 points. To be awarded the title of master sportsman, a player needed to score more than 70% of the points achievable by winning all games. Could 

a) 7 participants;

b) 8 participants;

have been awarded the title of master sportsman?

When leaving for work, a mother tasked Misha, Petya, and Vasya with the following chores:
a) sweep the hallway floor;
b) wash the dishes;
c) buy bread;
d) pay the electricity bill;
e) take out the trash;
f) vacuum the living room carpet.
In how many different ways can they distribute the tasks so that each task is done by one of the boys and each of them has at least one task to do?

Given \( a_{1}=1 \) and \( a_{n}=n^{2} \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \frac{1}{k^{2}} (n \geqslant 2) \), prove that:
1. \( \frac{a_{n}+1}{a_{n+1}} = \frac{n^{2}}{(n+1)^{2}} (n \geqslant 2) \);
2. \( \left(1+\frac{1}{a_{1}}\right)\left(1+\frac{1}{a_{2}}\right) \cdots\left(1+\frac{1}{a_{n}}\right) < 4 (n \geqslant 1) \).

Let \( O_1 \) and \( O_2 \) be the first and second centers of rotation of triangle \( ABC \), and \( O \) be the center of the circumscribed circle. Prove that:

a) \( \angle O_1 AB = \angle O_2 BC = \angle O_1 CA = \angle O_2 BA = \angle O_2 CB = \angle O_2 AC \);

b) \( O_1 \) coincides with \( O_2 \) if and only if triangle \( ABC \) is equilateral;

c) \( O_1 \) and \( O_2 \) are equidistant from \( O: O_1O = O_2O \);

d) The common value of the angles \( \angle O_1 AB, \angle O_1 BC, \angle O_1 CA, \angle O_2 BA, \angle O_2 CB, \angle O_2 AC \) does not exceed \( 30^\circ \); furthermore, \( \varphi = 30^\circ \) if and only if triangle \( ABC \) is equilateral.

The game takes place on a $9 \times 9$ grid of squared paper. Two players take turns; the player who starts the game places crosses in free cells while their partner places noughts. When all the cells are filled, the number of rows and columns $K$ in which crosses outnumber noughts, and the number of rows and columns $H$ in which noughts outnumber crosses, is counted. The difference $B=K-H$ is considered the winning score of the player who starts. Find the value of $B$ such that:

1) The first player can ensure a win of at least $B$, regardless of how the second player plays;
2) The second player can always ensure that the first player’s winning score is no more than $B$, regardless of how the first player plays.

Each unit square of a  $4 \times 4$  square grid is colored either red, green, or blue. Over all possible colorings of the grid, what is the maximum possible number of L-trominos that contain exactly one square of each color? (L-trominos are made up of three unit squares sharing a corner, as shown below.)

